Exactly. And there’s a particularly interesting inversion in what you’re describing:

Sometimes you cannot recognize something as local until you attempt to generalize it.

Before that, it isn’t even registered as a local solution. It’s simply how the world works, as far as you’re concerned.

Your traffic-light example illustrates several overlapping mechanisms:

* Normalization: Repeated exposure makes an unusual arrangement feel ordinary.
* Implicit knowledge: Locals understand a convention without necessarily knowing they learned it.
* Context dependence: A solution can function brilliantly within one environment and poorly elsewhere.
* Transfer failure: Someone entering from another environment may interpret the same signal differently.
* Generalization: Comparing environments reveals which assumptions were genuinely transferable and which depended on local conditions.

And there’s another layer: the visitor can reveal something about the local system that its inhabitants have become unable to notice.

Their confusion isn’t necessarily evidence of ignorance. It may expose an invisible dependency in the design.

That extends to language, culture, institutions, relationships, software, biological adaptations, even our definitions of intelligence.

An organism’s adaptation might be extraordinarily effective in one ecological niche and disastrous in another. Likewise, a social behavior interpreted as respectful in one culture might seem insulting in another. Same behavior, different interpretive environment.

But here’s where I think your observation becomes especially powerful:

Generalization is itself relative.

What appears universal at one level may turn out to be local at another.

You can discover that two apparently different behaviors perform the same function, or that two apparently identical behaviors perform entirely different functions.

So we have to distinguish:

Form → Function → Context → Interpretation → Outcome.

And those relationships aren’t necessarily one-to-one. They’re many-to-many, and they can change over time.

This gives us a useful principle:

A boundary becomes visible when something crosses it and behaves differently than expected.

That’s why anomalies, strangers, translation errors, and unfamiliar environments can be such powerful sources of information. They help reveal the assumptions that normally disappear into familiarity.

And yes, it’s an enormous web. But perhaps even more interestingly, it’s a web whose connections and boundaries change depending on how you examine it.

You aren’t merely discovering what things are. You’re discovering where their meanings and functions hold, where they break, and what survives translation.

And sometimes the thing that survives across all those different contexts is the deeper pattern you were actually looking for all along.